WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.280
If we were to reduce Greek&nbsp;
mythology to its key ingredients,

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:06.320
we’d have heroic quests, shapeshifting characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.040
vengeful Gods, bloody battles...and&nbsp;
some super intense love stories.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.720
These tropes appear again and again&nbsp;
throughout the Greek mythical universe.&nbsp;

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.915
But there’s three shadowy figures running the&nbsp;
entire show. Possessing powers even greater&nbsp;than the Gods, 

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:27.039
the Fates determine the destiny of&nbsp;
every shapeshifting, quest-weary, lovesick mortal.&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.360
It’s rare for the Fates to make&nbsp;
a sustained appearance in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.640
myth – but once you know how to&nbsp;
look for them, they’re everywhere.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.240
Today, you might associate them with&nbsp;
the crones of Disney’s Hercules.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:43.600
But these characters actually&nbsp;
blend the Fates with the Graeae,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:48.640
a trio of sisters who served the gorgons and&nbsp;
shared one eye and one tooth between them. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:53.920
real Fates keep their body parts to themselves,&nbsp;
but they did preside over life and death.&nbsp;

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.800
A closer look at their role unlocks an underlying&nbsp;
logic of Greek mythology – and raises important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.760
questions about power, free will, and the&nbsp;
human condition that remain relevant today.&nbsp;

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:10.160
The Fates were the fatherless offspring of Nyx or&nbsp;
Night, a primordial force in her own right who was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.640
greater than any God. Nyx produced a&nbsp;
variety of abstract concepts – I mean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.400
children – including Death, Doom, Sleep,&nbsp;
and Dreams. And none were so powerful as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:25.760
the three sisters known as the Fates.
The Fates spent every day spinning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.320
Their thread was the line of life itself,&nbsp;
a snip of their scissors signaled death.

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.600
In Greek they were known as the Moirai,&nbsp;
from the Greek word for share or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.400
portion. Appropriate for those who cast&nbsp;
lots to determine every human’s fate..&nbsp;

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:44.800
The scholar Hyginus wrote that the markings&nbsp;
on the Fates divination stones were the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.040
appearance of the vowels in the Greek&nbsp;
alphabet, reinforcing the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.760
we owe a whole lot to the Fates.
Within the trio, each Fate had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.760
her own role. When a baby was born Klotho, or&nbsp;
“the spinner,” spun the thread of their life.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:04.240
She was armed with a spindle and a small&nbsp;
loom, which was also called the book of Fate.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:10.640
Lachesis, or the apportioner, determined&nbsp;
the length of the lifeline. Her tools were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.960
a measuring staff and a globe upon&nbsp;
which she charted people’s fates.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:20.080
Atropos, whose name meant “the inflexible”,&nbsp;
chose the cause of death. And when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:25.760
time came, she severed the thread of life.
With her ominous scissors and scale of judgement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.880
she’s probably the most feared of the three.
Unsurprisingly, the Fates have long been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:36.240
associated with the march of time. Aristotle&nbsp;
imagined Klotho spinning out the present moment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:42.320
Lachesis determining the future, and Atropos&nbsp;
presiding over a past that cannot be changed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:46.320
They’ve also been portrayed in many&nbsp;
different guises and stages of life.&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.160
Plato imagined them as young women&nbsp;
sitting on celestial thrones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:53.680
singing in harmony, but they have also&nbsp;
been visualized as three older women.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.080
Other depictions split the difference,&nbsp;
showing Klotho in her youth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:05.200
Lachesis in middle age, and Atropos in old age,&nbsp;
this is classic Maiden-Mother-Crone iconography.&nbsp;

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.040
While their physical appearance&nbsp;
wavers, the fates rarely exhibit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.040
uncertainty. They were severe, stubborn,&nbsp;
and wholly opposed to interference.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.200
Setting aside the time the God Apollo&nbsp;
got them so drunk that they agreed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:24.480
delay the death of one of his favorite mortals&nbsp;
(oops!), the Fates could not be messed with.&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.080
They typically lurked behind the scenes&nbsp;
and rarely interacted with humans.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.520
One exception was when they&nbsp;
came to the hearth of Althaea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:37.280
and decreed that her son Meleager would die&nbsp;
once the last log in the hearth burned to ash.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.280
Horrified, Althaea quenched the fire&nbsp;
to prevent the log from burning.&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.080
Meleager grew into a skilled warrior&nbsp;
– but he also had a nasty temper.

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:49.360
When Althaea learned that&nbsp;
Meleager had murdered her brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.920
she reignited the log and let it burn.&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.880
In the Meleager myth, Althaea knows what the&nbsp;
Fates have in store - but that’s a rarity.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.920
More commonly, mortals and even Gods are&nbsp;
at the mercy of the Fates. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:06.160
events unfold according to their decree –&nbsp;
and people are left to pick up the pieces.&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:13.040
But isn’t this all very…fatalistic? If the&nbsp;
fate of characters is always inescapable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.560
why should we care? Where’s the drama coming from,&nbsp;
if the Fates have already determined the ending?

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.320
In fact, the tension comes from&nbsp;
how characters interact with fate.&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:24.960
Like when the Oracle in the Matrix:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.600
Reloaded tells Neo he’s already made the&nbsp;
choice; he’s just there to understand it.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:37.120
The trifold Fates as characters and the concept of&nbsp;
fate are often used interchangeably in Greek myth.&nbsp;

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.360
Whether mortals actually see the Fates or&nbsp;
not, the interaction between individuals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.520
and their unknown destiny is woven&nbsp;
into the fabric of Greek myth.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.640
Tragic heroes tend to be&nbsp;
obsessed with their own legacy,

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.040
lovers doubt whether their union was meant to be,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.840
and everyone continually ruminates over&nbsp;
what prophecy will come true and when.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:01.200
All in all, the problem of free will versus&nbsp;
fate comes up in pretty much every major myth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:05.280
And, to add an extra layer of complexity,&nbsp;
the question of how much the Fates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.560
trump the Gods is still being debated.
Let’s unpack the relationship between mortals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.240
and fate. Many Greek tragedies are built on the&nbsp;
folly of humans trying to outrun their fates.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.720
In the legend of Oedipus, King Laius of&nbsp;
Thebes learns that he is destined to die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:25.680
at the hands of his infant son. He orders&nbsp;
Oedipus to be put to death, but the baby is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.640
smuggled to safety in a neighboring kingdom.
As a young man Oedipus has his own encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:36.080
with fate, where an oracle tells him that he is&nbsp;
destined to kill his father and marry his mother.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:42.560
Desperate to escape this fate, he flees –&nbsp;
only to end up, fatefully, back in Thebes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.680
Sophocles’ play Oedipus Rex ends with&nbsp;
Oedipus cursing his own horrific fate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:50.480
but also realizing that he cannot escape it.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:54.960
The story of Oedipus reveals the&nbsp;
ironic components of destiny:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:06:00.960
by trying to avoid the fate you fear, you end up&nbsp;
making it happen--sort of like Final Destination.&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.000
In this example, The Fates don’t&nbsp;
actually appear to Oedipus. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.920
he learns of his fate via oracles, or mortals who&nbsp;
were gifted with the ability to see future events.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.000
If you think the communication in this myth&nbsp;
could have been clearer, that’s because Oracles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:21.040
were supposed to remain purposefully vague.
The Fates did not like explicit tellings of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:26.000
the future - when the oracle Ocyrhoe told&nbsp;
her father exactly how he would die, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.480
were so angry that they turned her into a horse.
The Fates also torment mortals in more indirect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:38.000
ways. Although they have the power to charm gods,&nbsp;
slay monsters, and travel to the underworld,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.240
most Greek heroes have a complicated&nbsp;
relationship with fate and free will.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.480
Hercules is continually depressed at&nbsp;
the prospect of his own early death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.040
while Jason loses any sense of his&nbsp;
own agency when his ostensibly heroic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:55.360
voyage leads to murder and dismemberment.
This is not to say that they don’t have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.360 --> 00:07:00.080
free will – it’s more that they’re plagued&nbsp;
by thoughts of their own legacy and demise.

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:03.760
Homer’s epic poem the Iliad shows&nbsp;
the Greek strongman Achilles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.920
grappling with this uncertainty. He knows&nbsp;
that his fate can go one of two ways:

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.360
leave the Trojan war and&nbsp;
lead a long, but anonymous,&nbsp;life.

00:07:11.360 --> 00:07:14.640
Or fight and perish in a blaze of glory.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:17.680
This dilemma would suggest that he does have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:21.840
a degree of free will – but the Fates&nbsp;
have already narrowed his options.&nbsp;

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.920
Achilles isn’t the only man of war&nbsp;
questioning his destiny. Throughout the Iliad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.920
warriors constantly debate who is in control&nbsp;
of the battle, the outcome – and their lives.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.440
This proves even more confusing when&nbsp;
the Gods get involved in the battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.520
picking sides and favorite fighters.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.520
But while their whims are fickle and&nbsp;
fallible, fate is an overarching force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.000
that sets the natural order of events.
In crucial hours of battle, Zeus is seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:51.360
to consult a scale. This suggests that a power&nbsp;
greater than himself determines the outcome of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:57.360
the war. We therefore might see Zeus not as&nbsp;
the maker of fate but as its agent or enabler.&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:01.680
At other times, the Fates step in to&nbsp;
decide serious matters. When Hades,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.320
God of the underworld, abducted the&nbsp;
daughter of Zeus and Demeter Persephone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:10.960
he argued that she should remain with him&nbsp;
because she had eaten the food of the dead.

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:16.480
Persephone maintained that since she had only&nbsp;
eaten six seeds of a pomegranate, this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:21.840
hardly enough to condemn her. She’s right.
Eventually, the Fates decreed a compromise:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:25.120
Persephone would spend six&nbsp;
months of the year in darkness.

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:29.680
One month per pomegranate seed is&nbsp;
a steep price, but even in the face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:35.280
of divine chaos, the Fates remain decisive.
Unwavering in their decisions, the Fates stand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:41.520
for the inevitable march of time in which the only&nbsp;
certainty is death. Not the cheeriest message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:47.040
I realize. But they’re also a powerful reminder&nbsp;
of the fundamentally mysterious nature of life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.040 --> 00:08:52.640
which can be as thrilling as it is terrifying.
The Greeks had these existential obsessions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:56.800
common with other cultures, who devised&nbsp;
similar personifications of fate.

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:01.760
In Norse mythology, the Norns are female&nbsp;
deities who sometimes appear in multitudes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:06.480
or are depicted as three tree-dwelling&nbsp;
giants who scribble humans’ fates on wood.

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:09.600
Albanian avatars come in one or three,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.760
and have been depicted as female spinners&nbsp;
who write babies’ fates on their foreheads.&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:20.400
In Korean Jeju mythology, a single goddess known&nbsp;as Kameunjangagi presides over fate.

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:25.040
Banished&nbsp;&nbsp;from her parents’ home for her crass humor and&nbsp;witchy powers, which she used to turn her sister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:30.640
into a mushroom, Kameunjangagi goes on to live&nbsp;
an independent life helping other outsiders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:35.840
She ultimately stands for the concept of finding&nbsp;
one’s purpose, in spite of life’s uncertainties.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:42.000
Although they were understandably feared,&nbsp;
the Greek Fates also offered solace and hope.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:45.680
Seeing as they were more powerful than the&nbsp;
Gods, it’s no surprise that the Fates were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:50.000
worshipped in their own right. They&nbsp;
were the subject of cults, monuments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.800
and inscriptions throughout Ancient Greece, while&nbsp;
the Sicyonians sacrificed black sheep to them. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:01.600
As harsh judges and watchful caretakers, the Fates&nbsp;remain a paradox. While they’re readily associated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:06.560
with the uncomfortable thought that death comes&nbsp;for us all, they’re also responsible for colorful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:16.421
lives and good fortune. Perhaps this duality is&nbsp;why they remain so resonant today – and, like&nbsp;life itself, there’s a magic to their mystery.

00:10:16.838 --> 00:10:19.520
Thanks for watching our new show Fate &amp; Fabled!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.040
There’s a lot more to come in future Fate&nbsp;and Fabled episodes. Dr. Z and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:27.040
I will be covering a lot more mythologies&nbsp;and ancient legends from around the world,

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.200
including Isis and Osiris, the&nbsp;
story of revenge and justice. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:34.800
why have cats have managed to slink their&nbsp;
way into even the most ancient stories?

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.120
And let us know in the comments if you have&nbsp;
a mythology you’d like to know more about!
 &nbsp;

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:45.840
See you next time!

